kind.prompt:
You are implementing a Blazor Web App for a logistics company that tracks shipments. The project is already scaffolded at `/tmp/sv-12b9c174215a4a26a9970c7af91d372b/ShipmentTracker/` using `dotnet new blazor -o ShipmentTracker -int Auto -ai -au Individual`.

This is an Interactive Auto, Global interactivity project with two projects:
- `ShipmentTracker/` (server) — hosts the app, has EF Core, Identity, API endpoints
- `ShipmentTracker.Client/` (WebAssembly) — all interactive UI components must go here

**CRITICAL Auto mode rules:**
- All interactive components/pages MUST go in the `.Client` project
- Components cannot access DbContext directly — must use HTTP APIs
- Both Program.cs files must register matching service interfaces
- The `.Client` project defines interfaces; server project provides server-side implementations

## Requirements
1. Dispatchers view a list of shipments with status and destination
2. Customers check their shipment status and estimated delivery
3. Role-based auth: "Dispatcher" and "Customer" roles
4. Remove template pages (Counter.razor, Weather.razor) but keep Home.razor, Auth.razor, NotFound.razor

## Implementation Plan

### Step 1: Shared Models in `.Client` project
Create `ShipmentTracker.Client/Models/Shipment.cs`:
```csharp
namespace ShipmentTracker.Client.Models;

public class Shipment
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string TrackingNumber { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Origin { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Destination { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public ShipmentStatus Status { get; set; }
    public DateTime ShippedDate { get; set; }
    public DateTime? EstimatedDelivery { get; set; }
    public DateTime? ActualDelivery { get; set; }
    public string RecipientName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string CustomerEmail { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string? Notes { get; set; }
}

public enum ShipmentStatus
{
    Pending,
    PickedUp,
    InTransit,
    OutForDelivery,
    Delivered,
    Exception
}
```

### Step 2: Service Interface in `.Client` project
Create `ShipmentTracker.Client/Services/IShipmentService.cs`:
```csharp
namespace ShipmentTracker.Client.Services;

using ShipmentTracker.Client.Models;

public interface IShipmentService
{
    Task<List<Shipment>> GetAllShipmentsAsync();
    Task<Shipment?> GetShipmentByTrackingNumberAsync(string trackingNumber);
    Task<List<Shipment>> GetShipmentsByCustomerAsync(string customerEmail);
    Task UpdateShipmentStatusAsync(int id, ShipmentStatus status);
}
```

### Step 3: Client-side HttpClient implementation
Create `ShipmentTracker.Client/Services/ShipmentHttpService.cs`:
```csharp
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using ShipmentTracker.Client.Models;

namespace ShipmentTracker.Client.Services;

public class ShipmentHttpService(HttpClient http) : IShipmentService
{
    public async Task<List<Shipment>> GetAllShipmentsAsync()
        => await http.GetFromJsonAsync<List<Shipment>>("api/shipments") ?? [];

    public async Task<Shipment?> GetShipmentByTrackingNumberAsync(string trackingNumber)
        => await http.GetFromJsonAsync<Shipment?>($"api/shipments/track/{trackingNumber}");

    public async Task<List<Shipment>> GetShipmentsByCustomerAsync(string customerEmail)
        => await http.GetFromJsonAsync<List<Shipment>>($"api/shipments/customer/{Uri.EscapeDataString(customerEmail)}") ?? [];

    public async Task UpdateShipmentStatusAsync(int id, ShipmentStatus status)
        => await http.PutAsJsonAsync($"api/shipments/{id}/status", status);
}
```

### Step 4: Register HttpClient and service in Client Program.cs
Edit `ShipmentTracker.Client/Program.cs` to add:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddHttpClient("ShipmentTracker.ServerAPI", client =>
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress))
    .AddHttpMessageHandler<CookieHandler>(); // actually, for Auto mode, just use:

builder.Services.AddScoped(sp => new HttpClient { BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress) });
builder.Services.AddScoped<IShipmentService, ShipmentHttpService>();
```

### Step 5: Server-side service implementation
Create `ShipmentTracker/Services/ShipmentServerService.cs` that uses EF Core DbContext directly:
```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using ShipmentTracker.Client.Models;
using ShipmentTracker.Client.Services;
using ShipmentTracker.Data;

namespace ShipmentTracker.Services;

public class ShipmentServerService(ApplicationDbContext db) : IShipmentService
{
    // implement all methods using db context
}
```

### Step 6: Add Shipment entity to DbContext
Edit `ShipmentTracker/Data/ApplicationDbContext.cs` to add:
```csharp
public DbSet<Shipment> Shipments => Set<Shipment>();
```
Make sure to import `ShipmentTracker.Client.Models`.

### Step 7: Create and apply migration
Run:
```bash
cd /tmp/sv-12b9c174215a4a26a9970c7af91d372b/ShipmentTracker/ShipmentTracker
dotnet ef migrations add AddShipments
dotnet ef database update
```
If `dotnet-ef` is not installed, install it first:
```bash
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef
```

### Step 8: Add API endpoints in server Program.cs
Add minimal API endpoints before `app.Run()`:
```csharp
// Shipment API endpoints
var shipments = app.MapGroup("api/shipments").RequireAuthorization();
shipments.MapGet("/", async (IShipmentService svc) => await svc.GetAllShipmentsAsync());
shipments.MapGet("/track/{trackingNumber}", async (string trackingNumber, IShipmentService svc) => ...);
shipments.MapGet("/customer/{email}", async (string email, IShipmentService svc) => ...);
shipments.MapPut("/{id}/status", async (int id, ShipmentStatus status, IShipmentService svc) => ...);
```

### Step 9: Register server-side service in server Program.cs
Add `builder.Services.AddScoped<IShipmentService, ShipmentServerService>();` in server Program.cs (need to add using for ShipmentTracker.Client.Services and ShipmentTracker.Services).

### Step 10: Seed sample data
Create `ShipmentTracker/Data/SeedData.cs` with a static method that seeds ~8 sample shipments with various statuses. Call it from Program.cs after `app` is built, using a scope to get the DbContext. Also seed Dispatcher and Customer roles using RoleManager if roles don't exist.

### Step 11: Create pages in `.Client/Pages/`

**Remove** `Counter.razor` and `Weather.razor` from `.Client/Pages/`.

**Update Home.razor** to be a landing page with links to the dispatcher dashboard and tracking page.

**Create `DispatcherDashboard.razor`** at `ShipmentTracker.Client/Pages/DispatcherDashboard.razor`:
- Route: `/dispatcher`
- `@attribute [Authorize(Roles = "Dispatcher")]`
- Shows a table of all shipments with columns: Tracking #, Origin, Destination, Status, Shipped Date, Est. Delivery
- Each row has a dropdown to update status
- Filter/search by status dropdown at the top
- Uses `IShipmentService.GetAllShipmentsAsync()`

**Create `TrackShipment.razor`** at `ShipmentTracker.Client/Pages/TrackShipment.razor`:
- Route: `/track`
- `@attribute [Authorize]`
- Has a text input for tracking number with a "Track" button
- Displays shipment details: status with a visual indicator, origin, destination, shipped date, estimated delivery
- Also shows "My Shipments" section that lists shipments for the logged-in user's email
- Uses `IShipmentService`

### Step 12: Update NavMenu
Edit `ShipmentTracker.Client/Layout/NavMenu.razor` to update navigation links:
- Home (/)
- Track Shipment (/track) — visible to all authenticated users
- Dispatcher Dashboard (/dispatcher) — visible to Dispatchers only (use AuthorizeView)

### Step 13: Add roles to Identity
Make sure to add role support. In server Program.cs, change `AddIdentityCore` to also call `.AddRoles<IdentityRole>()` and register `RoleManager<IdentityRole>`.

### Step 14: Build and verify
Run `dotnet build` on the solution to verify everything compiles.

## Important Notes
- The `.Client` project needs a project reference to nothing server-specific. The server project already references `.Client`.
- Use `@using ShipmentTracker.Client.Models` and `@using ShipmentTracker.Client.Services` in the Client `_Imports.razor`.
- For the status update PUT endpoint, make sure to use `[FromBody]` or accept the status correctly.
- The Customer tracking page should use `AuthenticationStateProvider` to get the current user's email for the "My Shipments" query.

Please implement all of this. After implementing, run `dotnet build` on the solution to verify it compiles. Fix any errors.

